{
 "id": "2026-11-18-c",
 "title": "Meteorites land red-hot? This one grew frost 🧊",
 "description": "You've been told meteorites hit the ground red-hot. On 4 July 1917 a meteorite fell on Colby, Wisconsin, and people who found it said it was quickly coated with frost on a hot summer evening. Why: a meteoroid enters the air at more than 11 km/s, but only a skin less than a millimetre thick melts, and that melt blows away, taking the heat with it. The glow lasts seconds; then the air brakes the rock to about 100-200 m/s and it falls 'dark' for minutes. Inside it is still cold from space, so fresh meteorites can be touched with a bare hand, and some even frost over.\n\nSources: Encyclopaedia Britannica (meteor and meteoroid); Wisconsin Geological & Natural History Survey (Wisconsin meteorites: Colby); AMNH (fusion crust); Armagh Observatory and Planetarium.",
